                <pb facs="0850" n="816" sameAs="frus:frus1948v02/pg816"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>In an undated letter handed to Secretary of State Marshall in Paris on October 6, not printed, Foreign Minister
                        Schuman reviewed French arguments
                    against the American proposal to suspend the dismantling of factories in Germany
                    pending the results of the Humphrey
                    Committee inquiry. Schuman emphasized that
                    an extended delay of dismantling could not be reconciled with current French
                    policy toward Germany and risked bringing to an end the inter-allied policy on
                    reparations (740.00119 EW/10–648). There is no indication in the files of the
                    Department of State that Secretary Marshall replied to the letter or discussed with Schuman any of the issues raised therein. During
                    a brief visit to Paris on October 12, Economic Cooperation Administrator
                        Hoffman called on Schuman. No record of the meeting has been
                    found, but evidence in the files of the Department of State indicates that
                        Hoffman urged a suspension of
                    dismantling and a delay of French requests for new allocations from the
                    Inter-Allied Reparation Agency until the Humphrey Committee had completed its review.</p>
            </div>
